  "Question": "The graph of \\(x^2-4y^2=0\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "a parabola",
    "B": "an ellipse",
    "C": "a pair of straight lines",
    "D": "a point",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Because the equation equals zero, factoring lets us separate its solutions into simpler alternatives:\n\\[\nx^2-4y^2=(x-2y)(x+2y)=0.\n\\]\nA product of real numbers is zero exactly when at least one factor is zero. Thus a point lies on the graph exactly when\n\\[\nx-2y=0\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x+2y=0.\n\\]\nThese equations are \\(y=x/2\\) and \\(y=-x/2\\), two distinct straight lines through the origin. Every point on either line satisfies the original equation, so these lines form the entire graph. The answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
